<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spanish American wars of independence</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1808">1808</a>–<a href="/years/1826">1826</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Spanish-American independence wars · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Spanish American wars of independence were the conflicts from 1808 to 1826 in which most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s American colonies won independence. They were triggered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s invasion of Spain. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> treat them as the end of Spanish rule over most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spanish-american-wars-of-independence.jpg" alt="Spanish American wars of independence" width="292" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ricardo Acevedo Bernal · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Congreso_de_C%C3%BAcuta.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Political independence was not the foreordained outcome of the turmoil in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a>, and there was little interest in it at first. In the 18th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourbon-reforms">Bourbon Reforms</a> increased the Crown's control and changed the language used of the overseas lands from kingdoms to colonies, and the appointment of peninsulars, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, to royal offices thwarted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creole</a> elites. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> ideas and the examples of the Atlantic Revolutions also spread through the empire.</p><p>The trigger was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In 1808 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> forced the Spanish royal family to abdicate and installed his brother Joseph as king. The Spanish world almost uniformly rejected this, but there was no clear answer to the lack of a king. Juntas formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and in 1809 short-lived juntas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sucre">Chuquisaca</a>, La Paz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> opposed the Supreme Central Junta of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>. Most Spanish Americans who supported the new governments saw them as a way to keep their autonomy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Juntas appeared across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a> in 1810 when the Central Junta fell to the French, among them the Junta Suprema de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> of 19 April 1810. Royal officials split between liberals who supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cortes-of-cadiz">Cortes of Cádiz</a> and conservatives. Those who favoured outright independence, calling themselves Patriots, used the juntas to promote their cause, and a war between Patriots and Royalists spread across the continent.</p><p>With the restoration of Ferdinand VII in 1814, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> turned its main effort to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, and by 1815 a stalemate had set in. In northern South America Simón Bolívar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-paula-santander">Francisco de Paula Santander</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-antonio-paez">José Antonio Páez</a> fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orinoco">Orinoco</a> basin and on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast. On 1 January 1820 Rafael Riego led a rebellion in Spain that forced Ferdinand to restore the Constitution of 1812, which let Rioplatense, Chilean and Colombian revolutionaries converge against the Viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. In New Spain Agustín de Iturbide began the process that led to Mexican independence. The Spanish fortifications at Veracruz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callao">Callao</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiloe">Chiloé</a> held out until 1825–1826.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The new national boundaries largely followed the colonial provinces. The republics abandoned the Inquisition and noble titles, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was not immediately abolished, and total abolition came in most countries only in the 1850s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creoles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestizo">mestizos</a> replaced Spanish-born officials in most offices, and creoles stayed at the top of the social order. The wars weakened the economies and political institutions and hindered economic development for most of the nineteenth century. Gran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America</a> collapsed after a decade or two. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> remained under Spanish rule until the war of 1898.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Spanish American wars of independence” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Spanish American wars of independence</b> (1808–1826) dissolved the mainland American empire quickly, leaving Spain with Cuba, Puerto Rico and the Philippines among its remaining major colonies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
